<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Phimai</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Phimai is a town on the Mun river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhon-ratchasima">Nakhon Ratchasima</a> province, north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>. It grew around Prasat Phimai, a large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> Buddhist temple built in the late 11th and early 12th centuries and linked by a road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a>. After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> in 1767 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thepphiphit">Prince Thepphiphit</a> made it the centre of a short-lived rival regime, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taksin">Taksin</a> destroyed in 1768.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/phimai.jpg" alt="Phimai" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Billbeee · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Entrance-phimai.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
